    {
      "type": "done",
      "answer": "Branch divergence occurs when threads within a single warp branch to different execution paths. Because a GPU uses a SIMD (Single Instruction, Multiple Data) pipeline to save area on control logic, it groups scalar threads into warps. When divergence happens, only a subset of the threads in the warp can execute at any given time, which wastes the SIMD pipeline's utilization [S1].\n\nThis reduction in utilization is illustrated by an example of intra-warp divergence where threads are split based on whether their `threadIdx.x` is even or odd, forcing the hardware to execute the two branches sequentially rather than in parallel [S3]. To increase SIMD utilization, programs should be designed to be divergence-free, such as by ensuring that all threads in a warp (e.g., `threadIdx.x < 32`) take the same branch [S2].\n\n**Source Pages:**\n- Definition and cause: Page 44 [S1]\n- Example of intra-warp divergence: Page 45 [S3]\n- Solution (divergence-free programs): Page 46 [S2]",
      "citations": [
